<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulysses S. Grant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1822">1822</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulysses S. Grant was an American general and the 18th president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. As commander of the Union armies he defeated the Confederacy in the Civil War of 1861–1865, and as president from 1869 to 1877 he oversaw Reconstruction in the South. During a world tour in 1879 he offered his good offices to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in their dispute over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant.jpg" alt="Ulysses S. Grant" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brady-Handy Photograph Collection, Library of Congress · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulysses_S._Grant_1870-1880_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Grant was born Hiram Ulysses Grant on 27 April 1822, the first child of Jesse Root Grant, a Whig supporter and fervent abolitionist, and Hannah Simpson. The family moved to Georgetown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, in 1823. When Representative Thomas L. Hamer nominated him to the United States Military Academy at West Point in 1839, Hamer altered his name, and he was enlisted as U. S. Grant. He served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> and married Julia Dent on 22 August 1848. After resigning in 1854 he had no trade, and seven years of financial struggle followed; he farmed near St. Louis and sold firewood on its street corners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the Civil War began in April 1861 Grant became colonel of the 21st Illinois Volunteer Infantry Regiment. His victories in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in 1862 brought him prominence, and in 1863 his Vicksburg campaign gave Union forces control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a>. After Chattanooga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> promoted him to lieutenant general and command of all Union armies. Grant fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a> through the Overland Campaign in 1864, and a nine-month siege of Petersburg followed. Lee abandoned Petersburg and Richmond after a general assault ordered on 2 April 1865 and surrendered at Appomattox.</p><p>Nominated unanimously by the Republicans in 1868, Grant was sworn in as president on 4 March 1869 and urged ratification of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifteenth-amendment">Fifteenth Amendment</a>. He appointed Ely S. Parker, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, as Commissioner of Indian Affairs, the first Native American in the post. His second term was beset by scandals, and the Panic of 1873 began the Long Depression. Leaving office in 1877, he toured the world for about two and a half years, meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Grant failed to win the Republican nomination for a third term in 1880. His savings were depleted, and a railway venture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> went bankrupt in 1884. That autumn he learned that a sore throat was cancer, possibly caused by his frequent cigar smoking, and he wrote his memoirs as he was dying. He died on 23 July 1885. At his death he was the most popular American and was memorialised as a symbol of national unity. His reputation suffered for a time under the Lost Cause mythology spread by Confederate sympathisers around the turn of the twentieth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ulysses S. Grant” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some officials fled to Fujian to seek Qing support, but negotiations, in which the former American president <b>Ulysses S. Grant</b> offered his good offices, came to nothing, and the islands remained Japanese.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ulysses S. Grant</b>, who served in it, called it “one of the most unjust ever waged by a stronger against a weaker nation”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/manifest-destiny">Manifest destiny · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>It was a diversion from Major General <b>Ulysses S. Grant</b>'s main attack on Vicksburg in the Vicksburg Campaign of the American Civil War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-17">17 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Robert E. Lee surrendered the Army of Northern Virginia to <b>Ulysses S. Grant</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ulysses S. Grant</b> pulled his Union troops from their position at Cold Harbor, Virginia, and moved south, leaving the battle to the Confederate army of Robert E. Lee.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-12">12 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>President <b>Ulysses S. Grant</b> signed the act setting aside Yellowstone's geysers and canyons 'for the benefit and enjoyment of the people'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-1">1 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Point Pleasant, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Wilton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.89,-84.23&amp;year=1862" aria-label="Point Pleasant on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ulysses S. Grant"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5383 4233h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5383 4233h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.89,-84.23&amp;year=1862">What was here in 1862? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;No terms except an unconditional and immediate surrender can be accepted. I propose to move immediately upon your works.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to General Buckner at Fort Donelson, 16 February 1862 &#183; original &#183; as printed in his Personal Memoirs (1885) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For myself, I was bitterly opposed to the measure, and to this day regard the war, which resulted, as one of the most unjust ever waged by a stronger against a weaker nation.&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Memoirs (1885), on the war with Mexico &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It was an instance of a republic following the bad example of European monarchies, in not considering justice in their desire to acquire additional territory.&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Memoirs (1885), on the war with Mexico &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I felt like anything rather than rejoicing at the downfall of a foe who had fought so long and valiantly, and had suffered so much for a cause, though that cause was, I believe, one of the worst for which a people ever fought…&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Memoirs (1885), on the surrender at Appomattox &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I feel that we are on the eve of a new era, when there is to be great harmony between the Federal and Confederate.&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Memoirs (1885), conclusion, written as he was dying &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am not aware of ever having used a profane expletive in my life; but I would have the charity to excuse those who may have done so, if they were in charge of a train of Mexican pack mules at the time.&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Memoirs (1885), on the war with Mexico &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4367/pg4367-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
